    content: "## Core Concept Have you ever wondered why variables seem to stick around even after their parent function has finished executing? That's the magic of closures! They allow functions to access variables from their outer scope, even after that scope has exited. ## Key Rules - Closures are created every time a function is defined. - A function can remember its lexical scope, which is the scope in which it was defined. - Closures can lead to memory leaks if not managed properly, especially in larger applications. ## 💡 Try This ```js function outer() { let count = 0; return function inner() { count++; return count; }; } const counter = outer(); console.log(counter()); // 1 console.log(counter()); // 2 ``` ## ❓ Quick Quiz Q: What is a closure? A: A closure is a function that retains access to its lexical scope even when the function is executed outside that scope. ## 🔑 Key Takeaway Understanding closures can help you write more efficient and powerful JavaScript code!"
